The **Human Development Report** of the **UNDP** ranks countries by the **Human Development Index (HDI)**, which combines:
- **Health:** life expectancy at birth (the number of years a newborn is expected to live, on average);
- **Education:** years of schooling (mean years for adults; expected years for children);
- **Income:** per capita income, adjusted for comparison across countries (US dollars).

The HDI runs from 0 to 1. Because it includes health and education, a country with a **lower income** but **longer lives and more schooling** can rank **higher** than a richer one. It is a broader measure than income alone.
